        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "3a9a4549-7313-54ee-9d90-f0868664db2c",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Antelope, Kansas; interior western and central North America; Lincolnville; Lost Springs; Pilsen; Tampa; Ramona; Burdick; Marion County; Morris County; Dickinson County; Chase County\n- Organizations: Centre USD 397\n- Products: Pronghorn\n- Concepts: antelope naming; colloquial names; parallel evolution; ecological niche\n\nFacts:\n- Antelope, Kansas, got its name from antelope grazing near the site where the first school was being built.\n- The pronghorn, Antilocapra americana, is an artiodactyl mammal species indigenous to interior western and central North America.\n- The pronghorn is not a true antelope, but in North America it is often called the American antelope, prong buck, pronghorn antelope, or simply antelope.\n- The pronghorn resembles the true antelopes of the Old World and fills a similar ecological niche because of parallel evolution.\n- Centre USD 397 includes Lincolnville, Lost Springs, Pilsen, Tampa, Ramona, Antelope, Burdick, and nearby rural areas in Marion, Morris, Dickinson, and Chase Counties.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "3dca8a1a-254d-5922-ab68-6f501c5bf118",
        "text": "Page: Antelope, Kansas. Sentence: Antelope got its name from antelope grazing near where the first school was being built.\nPage: Pronghorn. Sentence: The pronghorn ( ) (\"Antilocapra americana\") is a species of artiodactyl mammal indigenous to interior western and central North America.\nPage: Pronghorn. Sentence: Though not an antelope, it is often known colloquially in North America as the American antelope, prong buck, pronghorn antelope, or simply antelope because it closely resembles the true antelopes of the Old World and fills a similar ecological niche due to parallel evolution.\nPage: Centre USD 397. Sentence: The district includes the communities of Lincolnville, Lost Springs, Pilsen, Tampa, Ramona, Antelope, Burdick, nearby rural areas of Marion / Morris / Dickinson / Chase Counties.",
